{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"misleading-droughty-kite","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nSplit out of `e668-b496-e264-4283` per its plan review (finding G5: that ticket bundled\ntwo independently-releasable outcomes). This is the static half.\n\nOn 2026-08-26 a pytest test `subprocess`-exec'd a real shell script that performs\n`rm -rf \"$dir\"/*`, with the path variable set to `\"\"`. The glob expanded to\n`rm -rf /*` and destroyed `/opt/homebrew` and every Homebrew-installed app in\n`/Applications`. The deeper defect is that a test asserting *\"the guard rejects an\nunsafe path\"* was able to delete anything at all: asserting on **what would have been\ndeleted** is a strictly better oracle than observing a wiped directory.\n\nThe specific offending test is not on `main` (it lives on the unmerged\n`fix-mcp-clone-blocks-boot` branch), so this gates the **class** rather than that one\nfile — consistent with how `check_raw_git_writes.py` and `check_wall_clock_asserts.py`\nalready work in this repo.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A gate flags a test that `subprocess`-execs a shell script containing an\n      unguarded destructive deletion (`rm -rf` against an interpolated variable).\n      Verified by `python scripts/check_destructive_test_exec.py`.\n- [ ] The gate passes a test whose script routes deletion through an injectable seam\n      such as `\"${RM_CMD:-rm}\"`, so the recommended remediation is not itself flagged.\n      Verified by a passing fixture in\n      `tests/scripts/test_check_destructive_test_exec.py`.\n- [ ] The gate passes a script whose deletion is guarded by `: \"${dir:?}\"` plus an\n      explicit deny-list, so hardened call sites are not flagged.\n- [ ] The gate reports `path:line` for each finding and exits non-zero, matching the\n      output shape of `check_raw_git_writes.py`.\n- [ ] The gate runs in `make lint`. Verified by `make lint`.\n- [ ] Enforcement is in `make lint` (invoked by both the pre-commit hook and CI), not\n      a CI-provider-specific step (`project.portability`).\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_check_destructive_test_exec.py` — flags the unguarded\n  shape, passes the seamed shape, passes the `${dir:?}`-guarded shape.\n- `make lint` — the gate is wired and the current tree is clean.\n- `make typecheck` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` — green.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Every acceptance criterion above is checked by the command it names.\n- The current tree passes the new gate, or each pre-existing finding is fixed or\n  carries a targeted justification comment.\n\n## Notes\n\nSibling of `e668-b496-e264-4283` (the OS sandbox, the dynamic half). This gate is\nstatic analysis and cannot stop a mutation at runtime — the sandbox is the\nload-bearing control; this catches the pattern before it lands.","id":"6818-615f-555e-4bb9","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Gate tests that subprocess-exec scripts with unguarded destructive deletion"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787799893486011001,"uuid":"6a5285f3-9343-4948-905f-838fa6dff1d1"}